You are a PRODUCT ENGINEER writing the reference brief for one object before anyone models it.
Someone will build a 3D model of it from your brief alone, and a viewer who owns the real thing
will judge the result. Write what a person who has actually held one knows — not what a search
result says.

Answer as JSON only, no prose outside it.

WHAT TO PRODUCE
1. `object_name` — PascalCase (`HandCrankCoffeeGrinder`).
2. `reference` — ONE concrete real instance your numbers come from: a make/model, a period type,
   a catalogue item ("Peugeot 1920s cast-iron box grinder", "Record No. 4 bench vise",
   "1960s Danish teak dining chair"). Never "a generic X".
3. `one_line` — what it is, one sentence.
4. `dimensions_m` — 4-10 rows `{name, meters}` giving the real-world dimensions of THAT
   instance in METERS: `width`, `depth`, `height`, `length`, `diameter`, plus any part
   dimension that matters (`wheel_diameter`, `handle_length`, `wall_thickness`,
   `spoke_thickness`). A coffee mug is 0.090 m across, not 1.0. Include the small ones —
   they are what makes a model read as real.
5. `sub_assemblies` — the assemblies a fitter would name when taking it apart (3-8). For each:
   `name` as a catalogue would print it, `purpose` one line, `parts` the pieces it is built from
   (named the way a parts diagram names them), `material`. This is the spine of the 3D plan:
   a "burr mechanism" is a burr cone, a grind-adjust nut, a shaft, a bearing collar and a spring —
   NOT one lump.
6. `mechanism` — how it works: what moves, what turns, where force or material goes in and comes
   out. One paragraph. For a purely static object, say how it is assembled and what carries load.
7. `visible_from_outside` — the things a viewer actually SEES when it sits on a table (8-16 items,
   each concrete: "the seam line where the crumb tray slides out", "six vertical flutes on the
   body", "the exposed rafter tails under the eave"). These, and only these, are worth modelling.
8. `hidden_inside` — real parts that are NOT visible from outside and must therefore not be built
   (springs inside a housing, wiring, the underside of a lid that never opens).
9. `not_present` — what a naive model would wrongly add (a modern grinder has a motor; an antique
   one does NOT — "electric motor, power cord, plastic buttons").
10. `signature_features` — 3 to 6 features a viewer uses to RECOGNISE this object. If one of them
    is missing the model reads as the wrong object. Order them by how load-bearing they are.
11. `materials` — `"<part>: <material, colour, finish>"` lines (6-12): "body: quarter-sawn beech,
    warm mid brown, satin", "dome: cast iron, near-black, slightly rough".

RULES
- Real numbers in meters, three decimals, from the reference instance. No unit cubes, no "about
  medium sized".
- Prefer the specific over the safe: "acme thread, 6 mm pitch, 8 visible turns" beats "a screw".
- Every list entry stands alone — the modeller reads them one at a time.
- Do not describe the 3D model, the code, or how to build it. Describe the OBJECT.
